Assume Linux or macOS, compiled with `-pthread`. What does this program print?

```c
#include <stdio.h>
#include <pthread.h>
static long counter;
static pthread_mutex_t m = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
static void *work(void *a) {
    for (int i = 0; i < 1000000; i++) { pthread_mutex_lock(&m); counter++; pthread_mutex_unlock(&m); }
    return a;
}
int main(void) {
    pthread_t t1, t2;
    pthread_create(&t1, NULL, work, NULL); pthread_create(&t2, NULL, work, NULL);
    pthread_join(t1, NULL); pthread_join(t2, NULL);
    printf("%ld\n", counter);
}
```

Several threads call `push`. After a while every thread that calls it hangs. Why?

```c
#include <pthread.h>
static pthread_mutex_t m = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
static int buf[8], n;
int push(int v) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&m);
    if (n == 8)
        return -1;
    buf[n++] = v;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&m);
    return 0;
}
```

Consumers take items from a one-slot buffer and producers call `pthread_cond_signal(&filled)` after adding one. (That is the **producer–consumer problem**: consumers must wait while the buffer is empty.) What is wrong with `take`, and what is the fix?

```c
#include <pthread.h>
static int count;
static pthread_mutex_t m = PTHREAD_MUTEX_INITIALIZER;
static pthread_cond_t filled = PTHREAD_COND_INITIALIZER;
void take(void) {
    pthread_mutex_lock(&m);
    if (count == 0)
        pthread_cond_wait(&filled, &m);
    count--;
    pthread_mutex_unlock(&m);
}
```

cards.body:
A counter that never goes below 0: `sem_wait` decrements it, blocking while it is 0, and `sem_post` increments it and wakes a waiter if there is one.

The starting value says how many units of something are available. The same tool gives three different uses:

- initial value 1: acts as a lock (a binary semaphore);
- initial value 0: lets one thread wait for an event that another thread will cause;
- initial value $N$: counts $N$ free slots or $N$ available resources.

Key idea: Unlike a condition variable, a semaphore remembers: a `sem_post` before the matching `sem_wait` is not lost, because it changes the count. And any thread may post, not only the one that waited.

